+#ifdef VVV_MUSIC_STREAMING
+/* Streaming music (VVV_MUSIC_STREAMING, enabled for the PS2 build).
+ *
+ * The music blob is never loaded. At init only its header table is read and
+ * every track becomes a {file, offset, size} source. One worker thread owns
+ * the single open stb_vorbis decoder: it opens the track (this costs ~115 ms
+ * of EE time), reads through PhysicsFS and decodes into a bounded float ring
+ * buffer. FAudio's buffer callbacks (SDL audio thread) only copy out of the
+ * ring, so neither file I/O nor decoding runs on the game thread or blocks
+ * sound effects.
+ *
+ * Scheduling: the EE scheduler is strictly priority based and the game thread
+ * rarely sleeps (a frame that misses vsync skips the vsync wait), so a worker
+ * below the game thread starves (a track open took 12.8 s in PCSX2). The
+ * worker therefore runs above the game thread but cooperatively: after every
+ * ~2 ms of work it sleeps for 1 ms, so the game thread is never held off for
+ * long. Inside stb_vorbis the checks run from its read callback and from its
+ * sin/cos/qsort calls (the window/twiddle tables of a track open would
+ * otherwise hold the CPU for ~105 ms in one piece).
+ *
+ * Seeking must be cheap, so the blob has to be a STORED zip entry or a plain
+ * file (scripts/repack-data-zip.py); a deflated entry is detected at init and
+ * music is disabled with a diagnostic instead of stalling for seconds. */
